// concepts.memql
//
// Concepts in the authoring namespace (epic memql#954, issue #955).
//
// These are the graph-stored substrate for planner-authored `.memql`
// automations: a user Responsibility compiles into a *bundle* (one
// automation + its dependency closure), each member construct carrying
// its own `.memql` source + cached compiled form. The bundle is the
// ATOMIC unit of validation and activation -- you never half-activate
// a closure. Lifecycle (draft -> validated -> dryRunPassed -> active
// -> paused -> retired/failed) mirrors the staged gates in the design
// doc (docs/internal/design/authored-automations-954.md): Gate 1 isolated
// compile+bind, Gate 2 tiered behavioral dry-run, Gate 3 user approval.
//
// This issue (#955) defines the concepts + lifecycle mutations/queries/
// shapes ONLY. No engine wiring: the sandbox compile/bind harness
// (#956), catalog matching (#957), dry-run (#958), authored runtime
// (#959), and planner authoring loop (#960) build on top of these rows.

use identity.concepts.{ user }
use planner.concepts.{ responsibility }
use work.concepts.{ run }

/// An atomic, versioned unit of planner-authored DSL: one automation plus the dependency closure it
/// needs (logic / shapes / specs / traits / policies / mutations / queries / prompts), compiled
/// from a user Responsibility. The bundle is the unit of validation and activation -- the whole
/// closure passes Gate 1 (isolated compile+bind), Gate 2 (tiered behavioral dry-run), and Gate 3
/// (user approval) together, then activates together into the sandboxed authored-construct runtime
/// (#959). Never part of the core engine Init() path, so a malformed bundle can never brick the
/// cluster. Member constructs are v1:authoring:construct rows pointing back via bundleId.
@displayCard(primary="title", secondary="status", tertiary="responsibilityId", status="status")
concept bundle {
  ownerUserId         string! @description("v1:identity:user.id who owns this bundle (the Responsibility author). Per-row authz owner; server-stamped from actor.userId at create, never caller-supplied. Authored automations run under THIS user's authz envelope -- no privilege escalation.")
  responsibilityId    string  @description("v1:planner:responsibility.id this bundle was compiled from, when authored via the Responsibility intake path. Empty for bundles authored directly. Links the human-facing standing directive to its compiled artifact.")
  sourceRunId         string  @description("v1:work:run.id this bundle was post-hoc captured from, when authored via the capture path (epic memql#1160) rather than a standing Responsibility. Empty for Responsibility-authored or directly-authored bundles. Links the run that actually happened to its reproducible, inspectable compiled artifact, and is the idempotency + lookup key for the capture orchestrator (skip re-authoring a re-delivered terminal event). Was sourcePlanId until memql#5050: capture reads the tool calls a run recorded, so the thing it was captured FROM is a run.")
  title               string! @description("Human-readable name of the capability this bundle implements (e.g. 'Draft a reply when a refund escalation arrives'). Surfaced on management cards + the approval (Gate 3) artifact.")
  summary             string  @description("Longer human description of what the bundle does, generated by the planner design pass. Shown on the approval card alongside the dry-run trace + side-effect manifest.")
  status              enum("draft", "validated", "dryRunPassed", "active", "paused", "retired", "failed")! @description("Lifecycle. draft = constructs authored, not yet validated. validated = passed Gate 1 (isolated compile+bind) -- see validationReport. dryRunPassed = passed Gate 2 (tiered behavioral dry-run) -- see dryRunReport. active = approved (Gate 3) and registered into the authored-construct runtime; its automation is live. paused = temporarily disabled by the user or a circuit breaker without retiring. retired = permanently deactivated (superseded by a new version or removed). failed = a gate failed (validation or dry-run); failureReason carries why. Transitioned via the authoring mutations; the engine does not enforce a hard state machine here (the planner loop drives transitions).")
  version             int @description("Monotonic version of this capability. A user edit to the Responsibility (or a dependency change) produces a NEW bundle that supersedes the prior one via supersedesBundleId, rather than mutating an active bundle in place.")
  supersedesBundleId  string  @description("When this bundle is a new version of an existing capability, the v1:authoring:bundle.id it replaces. On activation the superseded bundle is retired. Empty for a first-version bundle.")
  reusedConstructRefs []object @description("Existing constructs this bundle COMPOSES rather than authors (the compose-first dependency strategy). Each entry: {name, kind, namespace, source: 'core'|'catalog'}. Net-new dependencies are authored as their own v1:authoring:construct rows; this list records the reuse edges for the dependency graph + impact analysis (#957).")
  validationReport    object  @description("Gate 1 result (isolated compile+bind, #956): {ok: bool, diagnostics: [{constructName, severity, message, line?}], boundConcepts: [...], unresolvedRefs: [...]}. Populated by recordBundleValidation. Empty until Gate 1 runs.")
  dryRunReport        object  @description("Gate 2 result (tiered behavioral dry-run, #958): {ok: bool, trace: [...], sideEffectManifest: {mutations: [...], aiCalls: [...], webCalls: [...]}, costEstimate: {tokens, usd}}. The approval artifact shown to the user at Gate 3. Populated by recordBundleDryRun. Empty until Gate 2 runs.")
  failureReason       string  @description("Populated when status transitions to failed -- which gate failed and why (e.g. 'gate1: spec specX references unknown field payload.foo'). Cleared on a successful re-author.")
  activatedAt         datetime @description("When the bundle was approved + registered into the authored runtime (status -> active). Null until activated.")
  retiredAt           datetime @description("When the bundle was retired. Null unless status=retired.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="responsibilityId", target=responsibility, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="sourceRunId", target=run, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="supersedesBundleId", target=bundle, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One authored DSL construct that is a member of a v1:authoring:bundle: the `.memql` source for an
/// automation / logic / shape / spec / trait / policy / mutation / query / prompt, plus its cached
/// compiled form. These are the NET-NEW constructs the planner authored because nothing existing
/// fit (compose-first, author-the-gap); reused constructs are recorded on the bundle's
/// reusedConstructRefs instead. After a bundle activates, a construct can be promoted into the
/// per-owner reusable catalog (catalogued=true) so later bundles compose it -- the catalog match
/// key lives in catalogKey (#957).
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="kind", tertiary="bundleId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept construct {
  ownerUserId       string! @description("v1:identity:user.id who owns this construct (same as the parent bundle's owner). Per-row authz owner; server-stamped from actor.userId at create.")
  bundleId          string! @description("v1:authoring:bundle.id this construct belongs to. Every authored construct is a member of exactly one bundle.")
  kind              enum("concept", "automation", "logic", "shape", "spec", "trait", "policy", "rule", "mutation", "query", "prompt")! @description("Which DSL construct kind this source defines. Drives which loader/validator the sandbox compile+bind harness (#956) routes it through. `concept` is the memql#3746 addition -- the anchor of the training epic, since a customer's domain arrives as nouns first and every other kind binds to one; a promoted concept row re-derives its canonical id at re-hydration from `origin`'s domain directory (it was the source's @namespace until epic memql#5375 retired that annotation), so `name` here stays the DECLARATION name like every other kind.")
  name              string! @description("The construct's DSL name as it appears in source (e.g. the automation/logic/spec name). Owner-scoped uniqueness; the authored runtime registers it under targetNamespace so two users' authored constructs never collide.")
  targetNamespace   string! @description("The namespace this construct registers under in the authored runtime (e.g. an owner-scoped namespace). Resolution precedence at runtime is core (sealed) -> owner-catalog -> bundle-local.")
  source            string! @description("The raw `.memql` source text for this construct. The single source of truth; the compiled form is derived + cached.")
  origin            string  @description("The TREE-RELATIVE path the bundle was authored against (\"planner/queries.memql\"), as the caller sent it. A promoted CONCEPT re-derives its canonical id at re-hydration, and the namespace half of that id comes from this path's domain directory -- it came from the source's @namespace until epic memql#5375 retired the annotation, at which point nothing on the row carried it and every promoted concept failed to re-register after a restart. Optional and additive: EMPTY on rows written before the field existed, which re-hydration reports as a refusal naming the path to send rather than guessing a namespace, because a guessed one is a canonical id nobody intended.")
  grammarVersion    string  @description("The engine grammar epoch this source was authored under (parser.GrammarVersion, S6 #2361), stamped at create. Re-hydration compares it against the running engine's grammar: a MISMATCHED stamp quarantines the row with an error naming the memqlmigrate rewrite mode instead of a silent degradation. Empty on legacy rows (treated as unknown; recompile decides).")
  compiledForm      object  @description("Cached compiled representation produced by the sandbox compile+bind harness (#956), so activation doesn't re-parse. Empty until Gate 1 compiles the bundle.")
  status            enum("draft", "staged", "active", "retired")! @description("Follows the parent bundle's lifecycle at a construct grain: draft while the bundle is pre-activation, active once the bundle activates, retired when the bundle retires. Lets a cataloged construct outlive a single bundle. `retired` means WITHDRAWN -- the boot re-hydration skips the row, so the construct never re-registers. For a promoted CONCEPT that is only one of the two withdrawal outcomes (see conceptRetired). `staged` is the middle tier (memql#3928): the row persists and is reviewable exactly as an active one is, and the boot re-hydration registers it OWNER-SCOPED rather than into the shared registry -- session-define, minus dying with the connection. Training is the transition staged -> active, which registers it shared and fires the cross-node broadcast; staging simply does not fire it, and that omission IS the tier.")
  conceptRetired    bool @description("Concept-only (memql#3756): this promoted concept was DEMOTED while rows already existed under it, so it stays REGISTERED -- name still claimed, existing rows still readable -- and new writes to it are refused. Deliberately NOT `status: retired`, which means the opposite thing on this row: a status-retired construct is skipped by the boot re-hydration and therefore never re-registers, which for a concept would make every row already written under it unreadable. A concept demote with ZERO rows uses status instead, because there is no data to strand and the name must go back to being claimable. Re-hydration replays this: the row registers the concept and is then marked retired, so a restart comes back retired rather than active. Resolution across rows is `a live row wins` -- a re-promote writes a NEW construct row with this false, and the un-stamped row is what un-retires the concept, so a stale stamp can never silently refuse writes to a concept its owner has re-promoted.")
  conceptDataStaged bool @description("Concept-only (epic memql#3974): the rows written under this promoted concept are STAGED -- they exist, they are addressable, and they are withheld from the ordinary read path until the concept is TRAINED, at which point they become live in place. The name says DATA deliberately, because `status: staged` on this very row is a different fact owned by a different epic (memql#3928): there, staged is a property of the CONSTRUCT's RESOLUTION SCOPE -- it resolves for its author alone and is not broadcast -- and `concept` is refused from that tier by name, since a concept has no owner-scoped resolution path and staging one would register an entry nothing could ever read. Here the concept IS registered shared, IS resolvable, and DOES accept writes; the only thing withheld is the VISIBILITY OF ITS ROWS. So this cannot be a `status` value even setting the collision aside: a status the boot re-hydration skips leaves the concept unresolvable, and rows under an unresolvable concept are UNREADABLE rather than staged -- the same argument conceptRetired records above, arriving at the same sibling-boolean answer. Visibility is a pure function of the CONCEPT and is marked nowhere else, least of all per row: measured against a fully-compressed TimescaleDB hypertable, this concept-grain flip is one insert at 11ms and decompresses nothing, while every row-marked variant either forces decompression (116-142ms per 10k rows, and hard-errors outright under timescaledb.enable_dml_decompression=false) or doubles version history and makes every later read of that concept 3-5x slower forever. Re-hydration replays it, and resolution across rows is `a live row wins` -- the same asymmetry conceptRetired takes and for the same shape of reason: a stale stamp resolving to LIVE costs visibility an owner can withdraw again, while a stale stamp resolving to STAGED hides the rows of a concept its owner has already trained, which is indistinguishable from data loss.")
  catalogued        bool @description("True once this construct has been promoted into the owner's reusable catalog (post-activation), so later bundles can compose it instead of re-authoring. Drives the compose-first reuse path (#957).")
  catalogKey        string  @description("Dedup / match key for the catalog (#957): a stable signature of what this construct does (e.g. kind + bound concept + normalized predicate), used by the similarTo-backed matcher to find an existing construct before authoring a new one. Empty until catalogued.")
  catalogMatchText  string  @description("The name-independent semantic text (kind + @description intent + canonicalized body) that was embedded for the near-match retrieval tier (#957). Computed by CatalogMatchText at promotion time and stored alongside the embedding (the vector itself lives in node_vectors keyed by this construct's id, vector_field='content') so similarTo can rank close-but-not-identical constructs for a stated need, and a re-embed can backfill from it. Empty until catalogued.")
  catalogedAt       datetime @description("Provenance (#957): when this construct was promoted into the owner's reusable catalog (the post-activation catalog-write path). Null until catalogued. Together with ownerUserId (who) and catalogedFromBundleId (which activated bundle) this is the full reuse provenance later bundles inherit when they compose it.")
  catalogedFromBundleId string @description("Provenance (#957): the v1:authoring:bundle.id whose activation promoted this construct into the catalog. Normally the construct's own bundleId, but recorded explicitly so the provenance survives even after that bundle retires (a cataloged construct outlives a single bundle). Empty until catalogued.")
  goalSignature     string  @description("The work spine's catalog key (epic memql#4966): sha256 over the NORMALIZED goal statement plus the SORTED input argument names, computed by component/work.GoalSignature. This is what compile's exact-match tier looks a goal up by, and it is a separate field from catalogKey because catalogKey hashes construct SOURCE per kind and refuses `automation` outright -- which is the kind a compiled goal is, so a goal could not be looked up by it at all. Empty on a construct that was never compiled from a goal.")
  reliability       float   @description("The trust ladder (epic memql#4966, spec section E `Promote`): how far this template has earned being replayed without a model. Climbs when a run whose fingerprints matched succeeds, decays on mismatch and on disuse. Absent reads as 0, which is the correct starting point -- a template that has never succeeded has earned nothing -- so there is deliberately no @default to stamp. NOTE the ladder ALSO exists on v1:actions:action and moves onto v1:skills:skill in epic A3; this is the TEMPLATE's reliability, which is a different subject from a skill's, and the two are not merged.")
  reinforceCount    int     @description("How many successful runs have reinforced this template. Absent reads as 0.")
  lastReinforced    datetime @description("When the ladder last climbed. Together with reinforceCount this is what decides whether a template has succeeded often enough to be offered back to its owner as a responsibility with its arguments as the form.")

  // The CERTIFICATION LADDER of a learned procedure (epic memql#5408, design
  // D15, D16 and D23). Every field below is optional and ADDITIVE: a construct
  // that was authored rather than learned carries none of them, and every
  // construct written before the epic reads exactly as it did. The DECISIONS
  // about these values are pure functions in component/work (Advance,
  // DecideServe); these fields only keep the state machine's state between
  // events, and the one writer is integrations/procedure, through
  // recordProcedure and recordConstructLadder.
  ladder               enum("candidate", "shadow", "canary", "trusted", "retired") @description("Where a LEARNED procedure stands on the certification ladder (epic memql#5408). candidate = lifted, not yet eligible to be compared; shadow = replayed in a sandbox beside the app on every matching recording, never serving a goal; canary = serves the goal with the app standing by to take over on divergence; trusted = serves the goal with no model and no app; retired = unused past the policy's window and served by nothing. ABSENT on every construct that was not learned -- an authored template is not on this ladder at all -- and a reader treats an absent or unknown value as NOT servable (component/work.ParseRung). Written only by recordConstructLadder.")
  preconditions        object @description("The learned INITIATION SET (design D16), component/procedure.Preconditions as JSON: platform (os, arch), tools (name to version, only the tools the procedure's own exec steps invoke), variables (name to unset or a digest) and emptyWorkspace. A predicate is kept only when EVERY recorded start agreed on it, so an absent key means the recordings disagreed or never measured it -- never that anything matches. A replay whose target cannot measure a learned predicate falls back to the app rather than running blind. Written by recordProcedure.")
  procedure            object @description("What a replay EXECUTES (epic memql#5408): the procedure TEMPLATE, not the rendered MemQL source, which is the auditable artifact beside it. Shape, version 1: {v, level, title, goalSignature, inputKeys, steps: [{tool, args, symbol}], holes, expect, inputMap, freeParameters, footprint, target, symbols: [{id, tool, template}], model, recordedFrom: {app, model, effort, sessionIds, runIds}}. steps[].args is component/procedure Node JSON; expect holds one component/work.StepExpectation per step; inputMap maps a free hole id to the goal input key that binds it; model is the procedure's own ProcessTree JSON, which the running check aligns the live trace against. Written by recordProcedure on the first lift and on every re-lift that changes it.")
  procedureHash        string @description("The VERSION a procedurePromotion approval pins: sha256: over the canonical JSON of {source, procedure, preconditions}. A change to any of the three is a different hash, and the decide handler recomputes the approval's artifact hash from THIS field, so an approval raised against an earlier version refuses rather than promoting a procedure the person never saw. Written by recordProcedure beside the payload it hashes.")
  shadowMatches        int @description("Consecutive shadow comparisons that matched the app in the CURRENT streak. A mismatch resets it to zero. Absent reads as 0.")
  canaryMatches        int @description("Consecutive clean canary replays since the procedure reached canary. Reaching the policy's canaryMatches moves it to trusted. Absent reads as 0.")
  distinctBindings     object @description("The binding evidence of the current shadow streak: free parameter hole id to the list of distinct binding DIGESTS (sha256: of the bound value, component/work.BindingDigest) the matching comparisons used. Digests rather than values, because a bound value can be anything the person typed. A procedure with a free parameter is proposed only once every parameter holds at least the policy's distinctBindings digests -- m matches on ONE binding prove nothing about the parameter. Replaced whole on every write, never merged.")
  failures             int @description("Consecutive failed replays on canary or trusted, a refused start included. Reaching the policy's failuresToDemote demotes the procedure to shadow; a clean replay resets it. Absent reads as 0.")
  insufficient         int @description("Replays that DIVERGED although every learned precondition held -- evidence that the initiation set is too weak to predict success. Reaching the policy's insufficientToDemote demotes the procedure to shadow. Absent reads as 0.")
  promotionApprovalId  string @description("The id of the OPEN procedurePromotion approval this procedure is waiting on, exactly as the approval writer returned it, or the empty string when none is open. The ladder never proposes twice while it is set, and it is CLEARED to the empty string when the approval is decided or the streak resets -- which is why this is a plain string and deliberately not a @relationship: a relationship canonicalizes on write, and the stored value has to stay the one the ladder compares against.")
  lastReplayAt         datetime @description("When this procedure last replayed -- a shadow comparison, a canary replay or a trusted replay. With the row's creation it is what the retirement sweep measures disuse from.")
  ladderReason         string @description("Why the ladder last moved, as one sentence a person reads in Nexus -- the Reason of the last component/work.Transition written.")
  ladderChangedAt      datetime @description("When the rung last CHANGED. A write that kept the rung and only moved the evidence leaves this alone.")

  // REUSE (epic memql#5414, design D24): whether a construct serves many goals
  // or one. The decomposer PROPOSES a reuse intent for each section it cuts,
  // the EVIDENCE decides the label (the reuse sweep, integrations/procedure),
  // and a person may OVERRIDE it -- the override is a version and the evidence
  // keeps counting, so Nexus can show both. Every field is optional and
  // ADDITIVE: a construct the sweep has not looked at yet carries none of them.
  reuse                enum("reusable", "goalSpecific", "accountSpecific") @description("What the EVIDENCE says about this construct (design D24): reusable once at least the feedbackPolicy's reusableAfterSignatures DISTINCT goal signatures of its owner have used it; accountSpecific when every use is tied to one account; goalSpecific otherwise. Written only by the reuse sweep (recordConstructReuse). ABSENT until the sweep has looked. The label a person sees is reuseOverride.label when that is set, and this otherwise.")
  reuseEvidence        object @description("What the sweep counted (D24): {goalSignatures: [string] -- the distinct signatures that used it, at most 20 kept --, signatureCount: int, accountIds: [string], uses: int, decidedAt}. Replaced whole on every sweep that changed it.")
  reuseOverride        object @description("A person's own label (D24): {label: reusable|goalSpecific|accountSpecific or \"\" to follow the evidence, by, at, version}. version counts every override written, so a later label is a new version rather than a rewrite. The evidence keeps counting underneath and is never changed by this.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="bundleId", target=bundle, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="references", field="catalogedFromBundleId", target=bundle, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One dependency edge in a bundle's construct graph (epic memql#954, issue #957). Construct
/// `fromConstruct` (kind `fromKind`) in bundle `bundleId` depends on construct `toName` (kind
/// `toKind`), which resolves from `toSource` (core = sealed engine, catalog = the owner's reusable
/// catalog, bundle = bundle-local). Edges are recorded at compile time so the catalog can run
/// IMPACT ANALYSIS -- 'which bundles depend on this construct' -- before a shared / cataloged
/// construct changes. This is the reuse-coupling mitigation from the design doc: editing a
/// cataloged construct re-validates its dependents.
@displayCard(primary="fromConstruct", secondary="toName", tertiary="bundleId", status="toSource")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept dependencyEdge {
  ownerUserId    string! @description("v1:identity:user.id who owns this edge (the bundle owner). Per-row authz owner; server-stamped from actor.userId.")
  bundleId       string! @description("v1:authoring:bundle.id whose construct declares this dependency.")
  fromConstruct  string! @description("Name of the depending construct (the one that references the dependency).")
  fromKind       enum("automation", "logic", "shape", "spec", "trait", "policy", "mutation", "query", "prompt")! @description("Kind of the depending construct.")
  toName         string! @description("Name of the depended-on construct.")
  toKind         enum("automation", "logic", "shape", "spec", "trait", "policy", "mutation", "query", "prompt")! @description("Kind of the depended-on construct.")
  toSource       enum("core", "catalog", "bundle")! @description("Where the dependency resolves under the precedence core -> owner-catalog -> bundle-local. Edges to `catalog` are the ones impact analysis cares about when a shared construct changes.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="bundleId", target=bundle, direction="outgoing")
}

// @no-displayCard: a singleton of six positive integers -- the certification
// ladder's thresholds -- with no field that names the row better than its
// literal id. Nobody browses to it: MemQL OS reads it once, on Settings >
// Procedures, and renders each value as a sentence rather than as a card.

/// The certification ladder's VALUES (epic memql#5408): how many shadow matches, across how many
/// distinct bindings, earn a learned procedure its promotion proposal; how many clean canary replays
/// make it trusted; how many failed replays -- or replays whose preconditions proved insufficient --
/// demote it; and how many days of disuse retire it. They are rows rather than constants so a
/// person can read the numbers the ladder is applying, and so the Go that applies them carries the
/// same numbers only as component/work.DefaultLadderPolicy() for an absent or unreadable row.
///
/// ONE ROW, AT A LITERAL ID: v1:authoring:ladderPolicy:primary, written by `seed ladderPolicy
/// primary` (dsl/authoring/seeds.memql) through createLadderPolicy. A global seed is re-asserted on
/// every boot on every node, fields the seed declares winning, so the row is refreshed the way the
/// cluster singletons are and an edit made anywhere else does not survive a restart. Every read pins
/// the literal id, so a stray row at any other id is never mistaken for the policy.
///
/// TIER: `@rowAuthz(clusterOwner, rankFloor="reader")` -- every signed-in person READS it, and the
/// WRITE stays where the cluster-owner tier puts it: the cluster owner, or trusted server-side Go
/// stamping internal origin (the seed materializer), with createLadderPolicy @serverOnly in front of
/// that. There is no owner field because the numbers are the deployment's, not anybody's. The floor
/// names `reader` and not `viewer`, although the two are one rung (rank 50, the lowest predefined
/// one): `reader` is viewer's alias, and it is the spelling the ENGINE'S COMPILED LADDER answers
/// (component/auth.RoleRank) as well as the seeded catalog. The load-time floor check runs at Init,
/// before the rbac catalog is readable on a fresh cluster and with no database at all under
/// memqllint, and there `viewer` would rank 0 and refuse the declaration. So the floor admits every
/// predefined role; the one caller it refuses is a custom role authored BELOW viewer, whose holder
/// reads nothing -- and the Go reader treats a row it cannot read as absent and applies the defaults,
/// which are the numbers this seed carries.
///
/// Every value is at least 1 (the schema refuses anything lower), so the row a person reads on
/// Settings is always the row the ladder applies; Go's normalization of a non-positive value to its
/// default exists only for the row that is absent.
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner, rankFloor="reader")
concept ladderPolicy {
  shadowMatches         int!  @minimum(1)  @description("m: consecutive shadow comparisons that must match the app before promotion to canary is proposed. The seeded value is 5.")
  distinctBindings      int!  @minimum(1)  @description("k: how many DISTINCT bindings each free parameter needs across that streak. m matches on one binding prove nothing about the parameter, so the proposal waits for k. The seeded value is 2.")
  canaryMatches         int!  @minimum(1)  @description("Consecutive clean canary replays that move a procedure from canary to trusted -- on its own evidence, with no second approval. The seeded value is 5.")
  failuresToDemote      int!  @minimum(1)  @description("Consecutive failed replays on canary or trusted -- a refused start included -- that demote a procedure to shadow. The seeded value is 2.")
  insufficientToDemote  int!  @minimum(1)  @description("Replays that diverged although every learned precondition held that demote a procedure to shadow at once: the initiation set has been shown not to predict success. The seeded value is 1.")
  retireAfterDays       int!  @minimum(1)  @description("Days without a replay after which a procedure retires, on any rung. The seeded value is 30.")
}
